{"name":"Epiphany","hex":"#dbc1de","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Epiphany","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A manifestation of the divinity of Jesus Christ, especially to the Magi (Matthew 2:1–12), but also at his baptism and the Wedding at Cana.","An annual Christian feast celebrating these events, usually on January 6, the twelfth day after Christmas."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A female given name."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Epiphany"}],"entries":[{"title":"Epiphany (feeling)","blocks":[{"text":"An epiphany is an experience of a sudden and striking realization. Generally the term is used to describe a scientific breakthrough or a religious or philosophical discovery, but it can apply in any situation in which an enlightening realization allows a problem or situation to be understood from a new and deeper perspective. Epiphanies are studied by psychologists and other scholars, particularly those attempting to study the process of innovation."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epiphany_(feeling)","wikidata":"Q2312056"},{"title":"Epiphany (holiday)","blocks":[{"text":"Epiphany, also known as Theophany in Eastern Christian tradition, is a Christian feast day commemorating the visit of the Magi, the baptism of Jesus, and the wedding at Cana."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Edward_Burne-Jones_-_The_Adoration_of_the_Magi_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/330px-Edward_Burne-Jones_-_The_Adoration_of_the_Magi_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epiphany_(holiday)","wikidata":"Q132001"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Flammarion.jpg/500px-Flammarion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Flammarion.jpg/1280px-Flammarion.jpg?utm_sour